{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790986577,"nonce":"yTAGW3ev9JygcWyrDDr0FGyEmpw=","title":"Explain concepts in Haxe terms, not by pointing at Rust or Blinc","message":"Many doc comments explain a type by naming its Rust or Blinc counterpart, for example `IntoReactive`'s \"Mirrors blinc_layout::binding::IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e\". Others say \"Blinc's `ThemeState`\", \"Blinc's `sd_shaped_rect`\", \"as Blinc's paint walk does\", or \"GpuPrimitive\". A Haxe developer who has never read Blinc or Rust learns nothing from these.\n\nEach public type and function should say, in Haxe terms, what it is for and how to use it. For example: IntoReactive is what a property accepts — a constant, a signal or a computed — and how each is bound. A Blinc reference may stay as a note for maintainers (\"ported from …\") after that explanation, never in place of it.\n\nWhere to look first, since contributors will meet these first:\n- the public surface: `ashui.layout` (IntoReactive, Node, Prop, PropertyId, DisplayList), `ashui.reactive`, `ashui.ui`, `ashui.style`, `ashui.theme`;\n- `ashui.core.render`, whose shaders refer to Blinc's WGSL by name.\n\nThe same goes for ideas used without explanation, among them SDF, squircle `n`, display-list records, owners, and the theme's revision signal.","files":null}]}